CRITERION 2 — THE CLAIMS ARE TRUE.
  (a) No symlink resolution in the package. MET.
      grep -rnE 'EvalSymlinks|Readlink|Lstat|Symlink' internal/services/storage/
        -> 3 hits, all in migration_decrypt_rollback_test.go (lines 234, 235, 314),
           all os.Symlink used to construct a dangling link as test fixture.
      Zero hits in any non-test file of the package: no filepath.EvalSymlinks,
      no os.Readlink, no os.Lstat anywhere in production code. Reading
      atomicWrite (storage.go:579-613) confirms the destination is the
      caller-supplied `path` verbatim: dir := filepath.Dir(path); staging via
      os.CreateTemp(dir, filepath.Base(path)+StagingSuffix+"*"); publication via
      `return os.Rename(tmpPath, path)`. os.Rename does not follow a symlink at
      the destination — it unlinks it — so the comment's claim is structurally
      correct, and nothing between WriteFile and the rename rewrites the path
      (WriteFile -> WriteFileContext -> writeFileLocked -> encodeForWrite ->
      atomicWrite, storage.go:349-389, all pass `path` through unmodified).

  (b) BEHAVIORAL PROOF — the load-bearing check. MET.
      On the scratch copy only, added
      internal/services/storage/zz_t6_symlink_proof_test.go
      (TestT6SymlinkContractProof), which: creates a real file in a temp dir
      OUTSIDE the data directory; constructs a real Storage via New(dataDir);
      os.Symlink's that outside file into the data dir under the data filename
      "transactions.csv"; asserts the pre-condition via os.Lstat that it really
      is a symlink and reads the target's bytes through it; then drives a real
      Storage write with s.WriteFile(link, newBytes, 0600); then asserts both
      documented claims. Command and full transcript:

        cd $SCRATCH/t6copy && go test -count=1 -v \
          -run TestT6SymlinkContractProof ./internal/services/storage/
        === RUN   TestT6SymlinkContractProof
            PRE : Lstat(link).Mode()=Lrwxrwxrwx symlink=true; ReadFile(link)="ORIGINAL TARGET CONTENT\n"
            POST: Lstat(link).Mode()=-rw------- symlink=false regular=true; content="NEW BYTES WRITTEN BY STORAGE\n"
            POST: target /tmp/TestT6SymlinkContractProof2794732470/002/real-target.csv content="ORIGINAL TARGET CONTENT\n" (UNCHANGED -> silently stale)
            dataDir entry: transactions.csv
        --- PASS: TestT6SymlinkContractProof (0.00s)
        PASS
        ok  	budget2/internal/services/storage	0.005s

      This proves, by observation rather than by reading the prose:
        CLAIM "the rename removes the link and puts a regular file in its place"
          -> Lstat mode went Lrwxrwxrwx (ModeSymlink set) before the write to
             -rw------- with IsRegular()==true after it; the test fails hard if
             ModeSymlink is still set or if the entry is not regular.
        CLAIM "the file the link used to point at is left untouched and silently
        stops receiving further writes"
          -> the outside target still reads "ORIGINAL TARGET CONTENT\n" after the
             write, while the data-dir path now holds "NEW BYTES WRITTEN BY
             STORAGE\n". The new bytes went to the link's location, not the
             target's.
      Also observed: the data dir contains only transactions.csv afterwards, so
      no staging file leaked.
      The scratch test file was deleted after the run
      (rm -f .../zz_t6_symlink_proof_test.go), and the whole scratch copy lives
      outside the repo; `diff -r --brief` against the main tree afterwards
      reported rc=0, confirming nothing was left behind.

